since the 5p charge on single-use  plastic bags came into force three years  ago our dependence on them has plummeted  which is why some are questioning the  need to go further it's been a huge  success 85% reduction amongst the  biggest retailers but there's still more  to go so we think that and asking  people's views on this by doubling it to  temperance it will be that extra  incentive for people to stop relying on  buying a plastic bag at their local shop  the Chancellor Philip Hammond is amongst  others reported to be unhappy about what  some view as another shopping tanks but  we've already seen that there's been  quite a decrease in the use of these  bags already so the idea that we have to  double it again it just seems to be  another I think unnecessary tax and  charge hitting consumers and taxpayers  there's no doubt we are using billions  fewer throw away plastic bags than in  the past and the money paid for those we  do buy has raised more than a hundred  and fifty million pounds for charity in  the process but price doesn't seem to be  the only go indeed major determining  factor the latest government figures  show the big seven retailers doled out  1.0 four billion single-use plastic bags  in the year 2017 to 18 down from 1.3  three billion the year before but if you  strip out the figures from Tesco the  biggest retailer which stopped offering  single use bags altogether  the total for the remaining six actually  went up by more than fifteen million  green campaign is a lukewarm about the  government's latest plan EMA priests  learned from friends of the earth says  plastic bags are not the biggest plastic  problem in our shops and charging for  plastic bags is really just the tip of  the iceberg we're using plastic in such  irresponsible ways as supermarkets or  some of the worst offenders of this they  need to provide more Goods loose in bulk  so that people can bring their own  containers and they need to stop using  plastic that cannot be recycled in an  easy way  environmental groups believe the easiest  way to purge single-use plastic bags  from our shops is not to charge for them  but to ban their use in the first place  dari boatman Sky News
 

